{
 "id": "vision_colour",
 "version": "1.0.0",
 "depends_on": [],
 "description": "COLOUR SALIENCE -- colour is the pop-out channel for the EQUILUMINANT target, where luminance salience is ZERO. Two rules generalise the eye's surround-relative difference (vision_lateral's lat_pool/lat_residual centre-surround) to the two cone-opponent axes L-M (red-green) and S-(L+M) (blue-yellow), orthogonal to luminance L+M: cc_contrast computes the CONTINUOUS opponent-space DISTANCE of a cell from its surround (the salience magnitude) and the same difference on the achromatic axis (luminance salience); cc_salience combines them INTERACTIVELY (lsal + csal + lsal*csal, the parameter-free superadditive form -- NOT strict addition; Bauer/Jolicoeur/Cowan) into Region.residual, which the unchanged vision_stream rule then streams a bounded budget over. So a near-equiluminant target (zero luminance contrast) with high cone-opponent contrast becomes salient via COLOUR and is streamed in, where a luminance-only salience MISSES it -- the 'ripe fruit against foliage' case. NOT categorical colour (the identity 'what' sign is the named frontier, out of scope). NO new Rust: reuses Fold/Neighbours/Div/Minus/Sqrt/Abs/Times + run_rules. In-frame colour coordinate is integer; only the contrast MAGNITUDE is continuous (the frame/continuum invariant). Flag-gated on ColourParams.{salience,colour}, both OFF by default (\u00a79). See VISION_COLOUR_PREREG.md.",
 "microtheory": {
